   "text": "I read the drawing as a cyclic hexapeptide lipopeptide of the echinocandin type, with a 10,12-dimethylmyristoyl tail. I worked out each stereocentre from the wedge and hash bonds, with hydrogens treated as implicit.\n\n**Side chain.** The acyl chain runs C(=O), then eight CH2 groups, then CH(CH3) (hashed methyl), CH2, CH(CH3) (wedged methyl), and an ethyl end. That is 10,12-dimethylmyristoyl, attached to the ring through an amide NH.\n\n**Ring residues, in order around the ring:**\n1. A 4,5-dihydroxyornithine. Its \u03b1-carbon carries the acylated NH, followed by CH2, CH(OH) and CH(OH)\u2013N. The label \"C\" in the drawing is that CH2.\n2. A 3-hydroxyproline.\n3. A 3-hydroxy-glutamine side chain, CH(OH)CH2C(=O)NH2, on the next \u03b1-carbon.\n4. A 3,4-dihydroxy-homotyrosine. Its side chain is CH(OH)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013C6H4\u2013OH, with a para-hydroxyphenyl group.\n5. A 4-hydroxyproline.\n6. A threonine, with CH(OH)CH3 on its \u03b1-carbon, which closes the ring back onto the ornithine carbonyl.\n\nI checked the SMILES for valid ring closures and for the chain length, and then assigned each @/@@ centre by hand from the drawn geometry.\n\nSMILES: CC[C@H](C)C[C@H](C)CCCCCCCCC(=O)N[C@H]1C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)NC(=O)[C@@H]2[C@@H](O)CCN2C(=O)[C@H]([C@H](O)CC(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@H]([C@H](O)[C@@H](O)c3ccc(O)cc3)NC(=O)[C@@H]3C[C@@H](O)CN3C(=O)[C@H]([C@@H](C)O)NC1=O"
